  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A strengthening ULT over the west coast\nwill develop a low pressure center this evening over central CA\nthat will migrate east into southern NV by Monday morning. This\nwill place eastern NV in its downstream and bisect the area with\nnortherly flow to the west and southerly flow to the east. Sunday\nevening sufficient moisture over northwestern NV will initiate\nshower activity over western Humboldt County that will push\neastward through Sunday night. Trace to 0.05 inches of rainfall\nare expected with overnight activity.\n\nMoisture advection mostly over UT with some spillover into \neastern NV will be the catalyst for Monday PM showers and isolated\nstorm activity. Dynamic forcing associated with the LPC\naccompanied by PVA in the downstream of the trough will initiate\nactivity over central and east-central NV Monday morning.  \nCurrent guidance keeps precipitation chances confined to higher\nelevations of northeastern Nye and southern White Pine County.\nRainfall amounts over peaks and ridges is forecast to be in the\n0.1-0.3 inch range through Monday night. The low treks eastward \nby Tuesday placing the area in its eastern (PFR) flank. By \nWednesday ridging will building into the southwestern CONUS and \nbring a potentially prolonged period of dry weather to the Great \nBasin. Under ridging a gradual warming trend will take place \nthrough mid to late week.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence continues for\nelevated fire weather conditions through Sunday evening. Low\nconfidence remains for shower and thunderstorms activity on\nMonday. Pops and Wx grids did a phenomenal job of showcasing the\nineptitude of the NBM Sunday afternoon where northwestern NV had a\nvoid of shower activity only in our CWA. SST and SerpISC were\nutilized to reintroduce Pops and shower activity back into western\nHumboldt County from 18Z through 00Z this afternoon. Moderate\nconfidence exists in a warming trend later this week.",
